## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of IAM DB authentication, RDS Proxy, and fine-grained access controls across accounts, plus a concrete testing plan.

## Key Concepts
- IAM database authentication with Aurora PostgreSQL
- RDS Proxy for serverless/large-concurrency access
- Fine-grained grants and per-service DB users
- Cross-account role mappings and network isolation
- Validation: latency, authorization, and failover tests

## Code Example
```sql
CREATE USER sales_read LOGIN;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA sales TO sales_read;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA sales TO sales_read;

CREATE USER orders_rw LOGIN;
GRANT INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA orders TO orders_rw;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA orders TO orders_rw;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA orders GRANT INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON TABLES TO orders_rw;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify IAM role to DB user mapping using Lambda? 
- What monitoring would you set to detect privilege drift or misconfiguration?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical latency engineering in multi-region Aurora Global Database deployments, focusing on per-tenant routing, cross-region caching, cache coherence, and DR validation.

## Key Concepts

- Aurora Global Database replication lag and cross-region reads
- Tenant-aware routing and regional cache strategy
- ElastiCache per-region namespaces and TTL-based invalidation
- AWS Global Accelerator and latency monitoring (CloudWatch, X-Ray)
- DR testing, RPO/RTO definitions, and security controls (encryption, IAM)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo routing decision by tenant
function selectRegion(tenantId, metrics) {
  const hotScore = metrics[tenantId]?.hotScore ?? 0;
  if (hotScore > 0.7) return nearestRegion(tenantId);
  return nearestRegion(tenantId, { cacheWarm: true });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate cache warm-up times for new hot tenants?
- How would you handle tenant migration between regions without SLA impact?
